+# Context management
+
+Run `pi -e packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/context-management/index.ts`.
+The extension works with standard Pi providers and needs no remote memory service.
+
+Use `notes_write({path: "summary.md", text: "Current goal, evidence, next steps"})`
+to keep concise working notes, then `new_context({})` to replace the working
+context after the current tool batch. The loop continues automatically. This
+does not create a session, undo filesystem changes, or generate a summary.
+System instructions and user messages remain available. The latest summary is
+included in context; other notes can be discovered with `notes_list` and read
+with `notes_read`.
+
+`history_search` searches the original session messages and tool results. Use
+its opaque `window_id` and `item_id` with `history_read` to recover original text.
+Search and note listings return continuation cursors. Reads return code-point
+offsets, so Unicode survives bounded reads. These tools return JSON text.
+
+Notes and window boundaries are stored as custom entries in Pi's append-only
+session file. Resume the same file with `--session` to retain them. This is
+session-local working memory, not a shared cross-goal knowledge store. The
+extension reconstructs the model input at Pi's context hook; raw history is
+never deleted. Filesystem-backed sessions provide persistence; `--no-session`
+intentionally provides only in-process state. Existing automatic compaction
+policy is unchanged. Branch navigation and multiple processes writing one
+session are outside this example's contract.
